window.docContents[76].resolve({"/rules/RG1004/#RG1004-established":{"contents":"No positive declaration depends on a compiler-trusting axiom: an authenticated native-proof axiom or the built-in Lean.trustCompiler, Lean.ofReduceBool or Lean.ofReduceNat.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Performance of the kernel replacement proof; cost claims are separate (R-COST).\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability* R-COST: cost claims name their domain and rest on an argument or bounded observation\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG1004: Compiler-trusting proofs require separate classification","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG1004/#RG1004-established"},"/rules/RG2003/#RG2003-established":{"contents":"Every claimed module elaborated from source without errors or warnings under the audit's build.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Fresh source elaboration unless the run is fresh: lake lint without --fresh is incremental and trusts Lake's build cache.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG2003: Claimed source must elaborate warning-free","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG2003/#RG2003-established"},"/rules/RG3002/#RG3002-established":{"contents":"Every reached non-native-runtime boundary of a checked surface has kernel-admitted correspondence.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Correctness of native-runtime primitives, the compiler or external code; a Lean equality does not prove external machine code. These stay trusted and reported.* That the executable roots are the ones the project intends to cover (R-INVARIANT).\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits* R-INVARIANT: the intended invariants, write paths and callers are all covered by proof-bearing interfaces* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG3002: Checked execution requires admitted correspondence","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG3002/#RG3002-established"},"/rules/RG4001/#RG4001-established":{"contents":"Every Lean fence in the checked tree has exactly one valid classification.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the prose around a fence describes it faithfully (R-DOC).\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability* R-DOC: documentation is complete and faithful to the declarations and requirements\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG4001: Documentation fences must have a valid classification","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG4001/#RG4001-established"}});